# Batch 2 — Pinning, drag smoothness, Task Manager, settings bugs

## 7. Pinned vs. unpinned ordering — clarify before building

Reported: "can't bring an unpinned app in between 2 pinned apps temporarily."

**Note:** this actually matches real Windows 11 behavior — pinned apps always
stay as one contiguous block in their pinned order; running-but-unpinned apps
are appended *after* the last pinned app, in the order they were opened. You
cannot drag a running app to sit between two pinned icons in real Windows 11
either.

Before implementing, confirm which of these is wanted:
- **(a) Match Windows 11 exactly** — pinned block stays contiguous, unpinned
  running apps always append after it. (Likely the current `TaskbarView.swift`
  logic already tries to do this per the dev log — "pinned slots always
  precede running apps" — so this may just need verifying/fixing rather than
  redesigning.)
- **(b) Deviate from Windows** — allow a running unpinned app to sit anywhere,
  including between pinned icons, as a deliberate feature choice.

## 8. Drag-to-reorder isn't smooth

The `NSEvent` local monitor approach (see dev log §3) makes dragging work
functionally, but the reported issue now is animation quality — presumably
icons are snapping to new positions instead of animating, or tracking the
cursor with lag. Fix target: icon being dragged should follow the cursor
1:1 with no lag, and other icons should animate their position shift with a
short spring/ease animation (~0.15–0.2s) as the dragged icon passes over them,
matching Windows 11's reorder feel.

## 9. Rename "Force Quit Apps" → "Task Manager"

Simple rename across UI (button label, panel title, any menu entries) in
`TaskbarView.swift` / wherever the force-quit panel is defined. No functional
change — current running-apps list UI is confirmed correct, keep as-is.

## 10. Task Manager: add per-app CPU / RAM / Network columns

Add three columns next to each app row in the existing list: CPU %, RAM
usage, network usage. This needs a new lightweight polling service (e.g.
`Core/ProcessMonitor.swift`) since none of the current files
(`WindowManager`, `FocusManager`, `PermissionsManager`) track resource usage.

Implementation notes:
- CPU/RAM per-PID: available via `proc_pid_rusage` (need each app's PID —
  should already be obtainable via `NSRunningApplication.processIdentifier`
  from the existing window/app enumeration).
- Network per-PID is harder — there's no clean public macOS API for
  per-process network usage. Likely needs shelling out to `nettop -P -L 1`
  (per-process network stats) and parsing output, or accept this column may
  need to be a "best effort" / omitted if it proves unreliable. Flag this to
  the person if `nettop` parsing turns out too fragile.
- Poll interval should be independent of and likely slower than the existing
  0.5s window poll (e.g. 1–2s) to avoid extra overhead — CPU/RAM/network
  numbers don't need to update as fast as window state.

## 11. Settings: icon size change doesn't re-layout properly

In `Settings/TaskbarSettings.swift` / `UI/SettingsPanel.swift` — changing the
icon size slider/value should immediately re-layout the taskbar (icon
frame size, spacing, and the host panel's height in
`TaskbarWindowController.swift` if height is tied to icon size). Currently
something in that chain isn't propagating cleanly — check whether the size
change is actually triggering a re-render of `TaskbarView.swift`'s icon row,
or if it's a stale-frame/layout-not-invalidated issue.

## 12. Settings: center alignment doesn't actually center

Selecting "center" alignment in settings should position the icon row
centered in the available taskbar width, same as Windows 11's centered
taskbar. Currently it's not moving from its (likely left-aligned) position.
Check the alignment logic in `TaskbarView.swift` — likely the icon row's
container is still using leading-alignment constraints/stack alignment
regardless of the setting, or the setting value isn't being read/applied at
render time.

## 13. Hover tooltip delay — reduce by half

Per dev log §4, the not-running-app tooltip currently appears after ~0.8s
(`IconTooltipPanel` in `UI/AppIconView.swift`). Change this delay to ~0.4s.

## 14. Custom hotkey for opening Start menu

Allow the user to set a custom keyboard shortcut to open/toggle the Start
menu (default is presumably the Windows key equivalent / a fixed hotkey
today). Needs:
- A hotkey recorder control in Settings (`UI/SettingsPanel.swift`) — click a
  field, press a key combo, it captures and displays it (similar pattern to
  how macOS system shortcut recorders work).
- Persist the chosen combo in `Settings/TaskbarSettings.swift`.
- Wire it into whatever currently registers the fixed hotkey — per the dev
  log, `UI/TaskbarWindowController.swift` handles "hotkeys" already, likely
  via the Carbon API (`RegisterEventHotKey`) mentioned elsewhere in the
  project. The existing registration should be re-done whenever the user
  changes the setting (unregister old combo, register new one), and should
  fall back to a sensible default if the user hasn't set one.

## 15. Start menu: typing should auto-search

When the Start menu is open and the user starts typing (no need to click into
a search field first), it should automatically filter/search the app list
live, matching Windows 11 behavior. Implementation notes:
- Likely needs a local `NSEvent` key-down monitor scoped to while the Start
  menu panel is key/visible, forwarding typed characters into the existing
  search/filter text state — similar in spirit to the local event monitor
  approach already used for drag-to-reorder in `UI/TaskbarView.swift`.
- First keystroke should both focus the search field (if one exists visually)
  and start filtering — no dead click required.
- Should not interfere with existing Start menu keyboard nav (arrow keys,
  Enter to launch, Escape to close) if that already exists.


